1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,513
Early in 1984, a woman named Jean packed

2
00:00:03,533 --> 00:00:05,124
her bags and called a taxi.

3
00:00:05,144 --> 00:00:07,541
She had been married for twenty years to a

4
00:00:07,561 --> 00:00:09,690
man who would not talk about his past,

5
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:11,590
and it had started to frighten her.

6
00:00:12,775 --> 00:00:15,621
Her bags were packed and a taxi was

7
00:00:15,641 --> 00:00:17,412
waiting, her husband later said.

8
00:00:17,432 --> 00:00:18,845
So he told her.

9
00:00:18,865 --> 00:00:20,994
His name was not Dennis Whiles.

10
00:00:21,014 --> 00:00:24,218
It was Georg Gärtner, and he was a soldier

11
00:00:24,238 --> 00:00:27,442
of the German army who had escaped from an

12
00:00:27,462 --> 00:00:31,095
American prisoner-of-war camp in 1945.

13
00:00:31,115 --> 00:00:32,715
The ski instructor, the tennis pro,

14
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:35,145
the man she had married, had been on the

15
00:00:35,165 --> 00:00:36,515
run for almost forty years.

16
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:40,111
About a year and a half later,

17
00:00:40,131 --> 00:00:42,417
he walked into an immigration office in

18
00:00:42,437 --> 00:00:44,064
California and gave himself up.

19
00:00:44,084 --> 00:00:45,711
He was sixty-four years old,

20
00:00:45,731 --> 00:00:48,017
and he was the last escaped German

21
00:00:48,037 --> 00:00:50,653
prisoner of war still at large in the

22
00:00:50,673 --> 00:00:51,312
United States.

23
00:00:51,332 --> 00:00:52,959
This is his documented story,

24
00:00:52,979 --> 00:00:55,285
and the story of why he ran.

25
00:00:56,975 --> 00:01:00,050
Georg Gärtner was born in December 1920 in

26
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:02,125
Schweidnitz, a town in Silesia,

27
00:01:02,145 --> 00:01:04,200
in the east of Germany.

28
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:07,825
In 1940, at nineteen, he joined the army.

29
00:01:07,845 --> 00:01:09,895
He was sent to North Africa,

30
00:01:09,915 --> 00:01:11,295
to serve under Rommel.

31
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:16,693
In May 1943, the German and Italian armies

32
00:01:16,713 --> 00:01:17,997
in Tunisia surrendered.

33
00:01:18,017 --> 00:01:20,170
About two hundred and seventy-five

34
00:01:20,190 --> 00:01:22,343
thousand soldiers were taken prisoner.

35
00:01:22,363 --> 00:01:25,840
Gärtner was one of them, captured in Tunis.

36
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,413
Like hundreds of thousands of others,

37
00:01:29,433 --> 00:01:31,647
he was shipped across the Atlantic.

38
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:34,997
By the end of the war, about four hundred

39
00:01:35,017 --> 00:01:36,858
and twenty-five thousand German prisoners

40
00:01:36,878 --> 00:01:39,463
were held in America, in around seven

41
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:40,208
hundred camps.

42
00:01:40,228 --> 00:01:42,441
Gärtner was sent to Camp Deming,

43
00:01:42,461 --> 00:01:45,047
in the desert of southern New Mexico.

44
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:47,300
There, he became the camp's translator.

45
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,844
In May 1945, Germany surrendered.

46
00:01:51,864 --> 00:01:53,628
For most of the prisoners,

47
00:01:53,648 --> 00:01:56,482
the end of the war meant one thing:

48
00:01:56,502 --> 00:01:57,196
going home.

49
00:01:57,216 --> 00:02:00,070
They expected to be sent back right away.

50
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:03,203
It did not happen quickly.

51
00:02:03,223 --> 00:02:06,930
Most worked on in America until 1946,

52
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:09,378
and some were then shipped to Britain and

53
00:02:09,398 --> 00:02:11,826
France, to work as laborers for up to

54
00:02:11,846 --> 00:02:12,744
three more years.

55
00:02:12,764 --> 00:02:16,200
In 1947, the American government was still

56
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:17,929
asking France to send home,

57
00:02:17,949 --> 00:02:20,351
by that October, the German prisoners it

58
00:02:20,371 --> 00:02:21,389
had handed over.

59
00:02:21,409 --> 00:02:24,156
For Gärtner, the problem was not when he

60
00:02:24,176 --> 00:02:25,194
would go home.

61
00:02:25,214 --> 00:02:27,290
It was where home now was.

62
00:02:28,525 --> 00:02:31,233
Schweidnitz had been taken by the Soviet army.

63
00:02:31,253 --> 00:02:33,619
It would soon become part of Poland.

64
00:02:33,639 --> 00:02:36,347
Gärtner later said that what for many was

65
00:02:36,367 --> 00:02:38,052
a moment of celebration was,

66
00:02:38,072 --> 00:02:40,097
for him, a time of horror.

67
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:42,825
He imagined what was waiting for him at

68
00:02:42,845 --> 00:02:44,530
the hands of the Russians.

69
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:47,598
He decided that the only way to stay free

70
00:02:47,618 --> 00:02:50,005
and safe was to stay in America.

71
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,903
He studied the railroad near the camp,

72
00:02:53,923 --> 00:02:56,027
and worked out when the freight trains

73
00:02:56,047 --> 00:02:56,633
came through.

74
00:02:56,653 --> 00:02:59,080
He told no one what he was planning.

75
00:03:00,425 --> 00:03:02,218
On the night of September twenty-first,

76
00:03:02,238 --> 00:03:06,061
1945, by his own account, he crept under

77
00:03:06,081 --> 00:03:08,397
the fences and wires, slipped past the

78
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:10,400
sentries, and climbed into an open

79
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:11,401
Southern Pacific boxcar.

80
00:03:11,421 --> 00:03:13,070
The train carried him west,

81
00:03:13,090 --> 00:03:16,741
all the way to San Pedro, the port of Los Angeles.

82
00:03:16,761 --> 00:03:19,765
His first stop, he said, as a free man.

83
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:23,863
He was one of more than two thousand

84
00:03:23,883 --> 00:03:26,343
German prisoners who escaped from American camps.

85
00:03:26,363 --> 00:03:28,468
Almost all of them were caught,

86
00:03:28,488 --> 00:03:29,884
most within a day.

87
00:03:29,904 --> 00:03:32,363
When the last prisoners were finally sent

88
00:03:32,383 --> 00:03:34,843
home, seven could not be accounted for.

89
00:03:34,863 --> 00:03:38,050
Gärtner was one of the men nobody could find.

90
00:03:39,725 --> 00:03:42,371
For the next year, by his lawyers'

91
00:03:42,391 --> 00:03:45,418
account, he moved from one West Coast town

92
00:03:45,438 --> 00:03:47,704
to the next, running from pursuers,

93
00:03:47,724 --> 00:03:48,846
real and imaginary.

94
00:03:48,866 --> 00:03:50,751
He washed dishes, cut timber,

95
00:03:50,771 --> 00:03:53,036
and picked crops alongside migrant workers.

96
00:03:53,056 --> 00:03:55,702
At first he called himself Peter Petersen,

97
00:03:55,722 --> 00:03:56,865
a Norwegian immigrant.

98
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:00,334
Then he settled on another name:

99
00:04:00,354 --> 00:04:01,035
Dennis Whiles.

100
00:04:01,055 --> 00:04:03,490
He perfected his English, got a Social

101
00:04:03,510 --> 00:04:05,243
Security card in that name,

102
00:04:05,263 --> 00:04:08,049
and invented a story about growing up in

103
00:04:08,069 --> 00:04:08,869
an orphanage.

104
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,532
He moved to Norden, in the mountains near

105
00:04:13,552 --> 00:04:14,420
Lake Tahoe.

106
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:16,640
In winter he taught skiing.

107
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:20,192
In summer he worked in construction and sales.

108
00:04:20,212 --> 00:04:23,156
Meanwhile, the FBI put his face on wanted

109
00:04:23,176 --> 00:04:25,453
posters, and the Army kept looking for him

110
00:04:25,473 --> 00:04:27,475
until 1963.

111
00:04:28,175 --> 00:04:31,873
In January 1952, a passenger train called

112
00:04:31,893 --> 00:04:35,335
the City of San Francisco was trapped by a

113
00:04:35,355 --> 00:04:37,258
blizzard in the Sierra Nevada,

114
00:04:37,278 --> 00:04:39,566
under about twelve feet of snow,

115
00:04:39,586 --> 00:04:42,643
with more than two hundred people on board.

116
00:04:42,663 --> 00:04:45,720
It took six days to free the train.

117
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:48,027
Hundreds of workers and volunteers dug

118
00:04:48,047 --> 00:04:50,335
through the drifts, and the passengers

119
00:04:50,355 --> 00:04:52,258
walked out to waiting vehicles.

120
00:04:52,278 --> 00:04:55,720
Gärtner later wrote that he joined the ski rescue.

121
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:57,643
Life magazine photographed the rescuers.

122
00:04:57,663 --> 00:05:00,355
Nobody recognized the wanted man among them.

123
00:05:01,975 --> 00:05:04,553
At a YMCA dance in Palo Alto,

124
00:05:04,573 --> 00:05:07,522
Dennis Whiles met a woman named Jean Clarke.

125
00:05:07,542 --> 00:05:10,779
They married in 1964, and he adopted her

126
00:05:10,799 --> 00:05:12,755
two children from an earlier marriage.

127
00:05:14,075 --> 00:05:17,619
He worked as a tennis pro and a ski instructor.

128
00:05:17,639 --> 00:05:21,182
With Jean, he ran a tennis club near Santa Cruz.

129
00:05:21,202 --> 00:05:24,390
They moved to Hawaii, where he worked as a

130
00:05:24,410 --> 00:05:25,815
construction estimator on military

131
00:05:25,835 --> 00:05:26,528
building projects.

132
00:05:26,548 --> 00:05:29,022
But there was always something he could

133
00:05:29,042 --> 00:05:29,735
not do.

134
00:05:29,755 --> 00:05:31,873
He could not get a passport,

135
00:05:31,893 --> 00:05:33,675
and it strained the marriage.

136
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,199
He believed his family in Germany had died.

137
00:05:38,219 --> 00:05:39,406
They had not.

138
00:05:39,426 --> 00:05:41,418
His parents had fled west,

139
00:05:41,438 --> 00:05:44,636
to West Germany, and were still alive in

140
00:05:44,656 --> 00:05:45,441
the 1960s.

141
00:05:45,461 --> 00:05:48,680
They kept his photograph on the kitchen table.

142
00:05:50,375 --> 00:05:53,869
By 1985, the couple were living in

143
00:05:53,889 --> 00:05:56,352
Boulder, Colorado, where he worked as a

144
00:05:56,372 --> 00:05:57,062
construction estimator.

145
00:05:57,082 --> 00:05:59,545
And his wife still knew almost nothing

146
00:05:59,565 --> 00:06:00,365
about him.

147
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:04,700
Early in 1984, Jean became so frightened

148
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:08,324
by his refusal to talk about his past that

149
00:06:08,344 --> 00:06:10,336
she threatened to leave him.

150
00:06:10,356 --> 00:06:11,947
The bags were packed.

151
00:06:11,967 --> 00:06:13,557
The taxi was waiting.

152
00:06:13,577 --> 00:06:15,590
Dennis Whiles told her everything.

153
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:18,463
She did not leave.

154
00:06:18,483 --> 00:06:21,529
Instead, she urged him to turn himself in.

155
00:06:21,549 --> 00:06:24,211
He began working with the historian Arnold

156
00:06:24,231 --> 00:06:26,894
Krammer on a book about his life.

157
00:06:26,914 --> 00:06:29,576
Krammer was the scholar who had written

158
00:06:29,596 --> 00:06:31,876
the standard history of German prisoners

159
00:06:31,896 --> 00:06:32,642
in America.

160
00:06:32,662 --> 00:06:35,325
Now one of those prisoners was sitting

161
00:06:35,345 --> 00:06:37,241
across the table from him.

162
00:06:37,261 --> 00:06:39,924
They called the book Hitler's Last Soldier

163
00:06:39,944 --> 00:06:40,744
in America.

164
00:06:42,475 --> 00:06:45,814
In September 1985, Gärtner walked into the

165
00:06:45,834 --> 00:06:47,579
office of the Immigration and

166
00:06:47,599 --> 00:06:49,345
Naturalization Service on Terminal Island,

167
00:06:49,365 --> 00:06:52,169
in San Pedro, the same port where his

168
00:06:52,189 --> 00:06:54,287
boxcar had arrived forty years earlier.

169
00:06:54,307 --> 00:06:56,758
In front of reporters, with Jean beside

170
00:06:56,778 --> 00:06:59,583
him, he told his story, and there were

171
00:06:59,603 --> 00:07:01,015
tears on his cheeks.

172
00:07:02,325 --> 00:07:04,433
The timing was not an accident.

173
00:07:04,453 --> 00:07:07,270
His surrender was set to coincide with the

174
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:09,752
release of the book, and the publisher

175
00:07:09,772 --> 00:07:10,462
said so.

176
00:07:10,482 --> 00:07:12,235
On national television, he surrendered

177
00:07:12,255 --> 00:07:15,426
once more, this time to the host of the

178
00:07:15,446 --> 00:07:16,865
Today show, Bryant Gumbel.

179
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:21,522
Then came the question of what to do with him.

180
00:07:21,542 --> 00:07:23,243
The regional commissioner of the

181
00:07:23,263 --> 00:07:24,963
immigration service said he was

182
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:26,340
technically a deportable alien.

183
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,093
But he had been married to an American

184
00:07:29,113 --> 00:07:31,158
citizen for more than twenty years,

185
00:07:31,178 --> 00:07:33,912
and his chances of being allowed to stay

186
00:07:33,932 --> 00:07:34,732
were excellent.

187
00:07:36,375 --> 00:07:38,194
He was never sent away.

188
00:07:38,214 --> 00:07:40,769
He was not prosecuted for the escape.

189
00:07:40,789 --> 00:07:42,975
He went on living in Colorado,

190
00:07:42,995 --> 00:07:46,286
and in 2009, he became a citizen of the

191
00:07:46,306 --> 00:07:49,228
United States, the country he had run into

192
00:07:49,248 --> 00:07:51,455
as a prisoner sixty-four years earlier.

193
00:07:52,725 --> 00:07:55,053
Georg Gärtner died in January 2013,

194
00:07:55,072 --> 00:07:56,618
in Colorado, at ninety-two.

195
00:07:56,638 --> 00:07:59,748
For forty years, the files had called him

196
00:07:59,768 --> 00:08:01,704
an escaped prisoner of war.

197
00:08:01,724 --> 00:08:05,245
In the end, he was simply the last one.

198
00:08:06,475 --> 00:08:08,628
If someone in your family came to America

199
00:08:08,648 --> 00:08:10,257
as a prisoner, or met one,

200
00:08:10,277 --> 00:08:11,635
tell us in the comments.

201
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:16,724
In 1945, almost every German prisoner in

202
00:08:16,744 --> 00:08:18,997
America was waiting for the same thing:

203
00:08:19,017 --> 00:08:22,245
a train and a ship that would take him home.

204
00:08:22,265 --> 00:08:24,193
One man looked at the map,

205
00:08:24,213 --> 00:08:27,116
saw what home had become, and jumped on a

206
00:08:27,136 --> 00:08:28,740
train going the other way.

207
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:31,013
He spent forty years as someone else.

208
00:08:31,033 --> 00:08:33,611
It took a packed suitcase and a waiting

209
00:08:33,631 --> 00:08:35,580
taxi to bring Georg Gärtner back.

